The {{nihongo|'''Okaiken Railways'''|岡以県鉄道|Okaiken Tetsudō}}, commonly referred to as '''OR''' or the '''OR Group''', is the state-owned national railway system of [[Okaiken]]. Unlike the fully privatized railway models seen in other nations, OR is structured as an Imperial Crown Corporation, jointly owned by the Okaiken Government and the National Pension Fund.

The network is divided geographically among three major operating subsidiaries corresponding to the nation's three main landmasses: '''Harujima''' (Northwest), '''Ishijima''' (Northeast), and '''Yagikoshu''' (South). While these entities have operational autonomy, they are centrally managed by the OR Group Directorate to ensure national cohesion, fare stabilization, and strategic infrastructure maintenance.

The OR Group operates the high-speed [[Shinkansen]] network, intercity limited expresses, and the urban commuter networks in major metropolises including the capital, [[Okami]].

==History==
===The Reform of 1987===
Prior to 1987, the network was operated by the Okaiken National Railways (ONR), a direct ministry-level bureau. While ONR was instrumental in Okaiken's post-war modernization, it suffered from bureaucratic bloating and significant debt accumulation.

In 1987, rather than pursuing full privatization which was deemed a risk to national security and rural connectivity, the government reorganized the ONR into the Okaiken Railways Group. The ownership was transferred to a holding company held 51% by the Imperial Treasury and 49% by the National Pension Fund. This structure mandates that profits are reinvested into the network or used to bolster the nation's social security, rather than distributed to private shareholders.

