==Shinkansen Network==
The backbone of the OR network is the [[Shinkansen]] system, which connects the three major islands via undersea tunnels and massive bridges. The three lines converge near the geographical center of the archipelago. The OR Group as a whole operates and maintains the Shinkansen Network to allow for better consistency of timetable planning.

===Harujima Shinkansen (OR Harujima)===
* '''Route:''' [[Shinagawa]] — [[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chuo]] — [[Anjo-Saikawa]] — [[Toyozawa]]
* '''Color:''' Deep Red
* '''Description:''' The busiest line in the system, connecting the northern port of Shinagawa with the capital city of [[Okami]] and continuing south to the central junction of Toyozawa. It is the primary artery for business travel.

===Ishijima Shinkansen (OR Ishijima)===
* '''Route:''' [[Aizawa]] — [[Minatojima]] — [[Toyozawa]]
* '''Color:'''  Blue
* '''Description:''' Runs along the western spine of Ishijima, connecting the northwestern metropolis of Aizawa with Toyozawa. This line is famous for its scenic coastal sections.

===Minami-Yagikoshu Shinkansen (OR Yagikoshu)===
* '''Route:''' [[Toyozawa]] — [[Mito]] — [[Sakata]] — [[Aomori (Yagikoshu)|Aomori]]
* '''Color:''' Forest Green
* '''Description:''' Extending southward from Toyozawa, this line penetrates the mountainous terrain of the Yagikoshu region, connecting the southern agricultural hubs to the national grid.

==Logo and branding==
The corporate identity of Okaiken Railways (OR) is anchored by its distinctive and bold logomark. The primary color utilized is a deep red—the national color—which imbues the mark with a sense of official authority, stability, and robustness appropriate for the country's national carrier.

The design itself is a stylized, geometric ligature of the letters "O" and "R". The seamless connection between the circular left side and the arching right side suggests unity and a continuous, uninterrupted transportation network across [[Okaiken]]. The design also conveys a strong sense of forward momentum; the diagonal, outward-sweeping leg of the "R" implies speed and progress.

Furthermore, the abstract geometry incorporates clever railway imagery. The thick circle of the "O" with its central negative space strongly resembles a train wheel or bogie, while simultaneously evoking the perspective of looking into a tunnel.

Perhaps the most significant design element is its homage to railway heritage. The bold, abstract silhouette cleverly captures the form of a classic [[steam locomotive]] in motion. The "O" section represents the front boiler plate and driving wheel, the arching top of the "R" suggests the boiler tank of an old streamliner like the [[Nanpu (train)|Nanpu]] , and the diagonal leg mimics the front [[cowcatcher]]. This design choice effectively bridges the gap between the Empire's historical foundation in rail transport and its modern, future-facing infrastructure.

